"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[751],{8751:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"methods","title":"Methods","overviewSimple":"Imagine you have a magic button that does a specific task whenever you press it \u2014 like turning on a light or playing music. A method in Java is like that button: you create it once, and you can use it many times whenever you need that task done.","overviewTechnical":["Methods in Java are reusable blocks of code that perform a specific task.","A method has a return type, a name, and optionally parameters.","Java supports both static methods (called without an object) and instance methods (require an object).","Method overloading allows multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists.","Methods enable code reusability, modularity, and better organization."],"story":"Ravi created a special button on his robot labeled \'dance\'. Whenever he pressed it, the robot danced exactly the same way. Instead of repeating instructions every time, he used one magic button \u2014 just like Java methods.","realWorldAnalogy":"A method is like a microwave preset button. You hit \'popcorn\', and it always performs the same sequence of actions.","keyPoints":["Methods reduce code duplication","Can have parameters and return values","Can be static or instance methods","Method overloading allows flexible usage"],"exampleSimple":"int add(int a, int b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n}\\nSystem.out.println(add(5, 3)); // prints 8","exampleTechnical":["public class MathUtil {\\n    public static int square(int x) {\\n        return x * x;\\n    }\\n\\n    public double divide(double a, double b) {\\n        if (b == 0) return 0;\\n        return a / b;\\n    }\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        System.out.println(MathUtil.square(4));\\n\\n        MathUtil util = new MathUtil();\\n        System.out.println(util.divide(10, 2));\\n    }\\n}","public class OverloadDemo {\\n    void greet() {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Hello!\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    void greet(String name) {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Hello, \\" + name + \\"!\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        OverloadDemo d = new OverloadDemo();\\n        d.greet();\\n        d.greet(\\"John\\");\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["Java does not support default arguments directly (like Python or C++), but method overloading is used instead.","Return type does not participate in method overloading resolution.","Methods help enforce the DRY principle (Don\'t Repeat Yourself)."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Use clear and simple method names","Keep each method focused on one task"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Follow naming conventions: verbs for methods (calculateTotal, printData)","Avoid long parameter lists \u2014 use objects or builders if needed","Document complex methods using Javadoc","Keep methods small and modular for better readability and testing"]}}')}}]);